---
import { basename } from "node:path";

import CodeSnippet from "../components/CodeSnippet.astro";
import HomeIcon from "../components/HomeIcon.astro";
import { posts } from "../data/posts";
import Layout from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { dateFormatter } from "../utils";
import Link from "../components/Link.astro";

const id = basename(import.meta.url, ".astro");
const post = posts.find((p) => p.id === id);

if (!post) throw new Error(`No post found with id "${id}".`);

const config = `
{
  "extensions.autoCheckUpdates": false,
  "extensions.autoUpdate": false
}
`.trim();

const saveCommand = "code --list-extensions --show-versions > vscode_extensions.txt";

const installCommand = "xargs --max-lines=1 code --install-extension < vscode_extensions.txt";
---

<Layout
  title={post.title}
  description={post.description}
  type="article"
  publishedTime={post.createdAt}
  modifiedTime={post.lastUpdatedAt}
>
  <header class="mb-12">
    <a
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 font-medium focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-blue-500"
      aria-label="Homepage"
      href="/"><HomeIcon />Patches</a
    >
  </header>

  <main>
    <article>
      <header>
        <h1 class="text-xl font-medium">{post.title}</h1>

        <p>
          Published on <time datetime={post.createdAt}>{dateFormatter.format(new Date(post.createdAt))}</time>.{
            post.lastUpdatedAt && (
              <>
                {" "}
                Last updated on{" "}
                <time datetime={post.lastUpdatedAt}>{dateFormatter.format(new Date(post.lastUpdatedAt))}</time>.
              </>
            )
          }
        </p>
      </header>

      <p>
        VS Code extensions are <Link
          url="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/supporting/faq#_how-do-i-opt-out-of-vs-code-autoupdates"
          >updated automatically by default</Link
        >. Although I am careful when installing a new extension, I used to manage them less strictly than project
        dependencies. Here are the steps for my current workflow:
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-medium">Disable automatic extension updates</h2>

      <p>
        Add the following settings to the <Link
          url="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/configure/settings#_user-settings"
          >VS Code user (global) settings</Link
        >:
      </p>

      <CodeSnippet code={config} lang="json" />

      <h2 class="font-medium">Save a pseudo-lockfile of installed extensions</h2>

      <p>First, review the installed extensions and uninstall the unused ones. Then run the following command:</p>

      <CodeSnippet code={saveCommand} lang="bash" />

      <p>
        Keep the generated file somewhere, like in a <Link url="https://github.com/joaopalmeiro/dotfiles"
          >dotfiles repo</Link
        >. This file makes it easier to get VS Code up and running after a fresh install (and if you don't use <Link
          url="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/configure/settings-sync">Settings Sync</Link
        >). To do so, run the following command:
      </p>

      <CodeSnippet code={installCommand} lang="bash" />

      <h2 class="font-medium">Update extensions manually</h2>

      <p>
        Run the <i>Extensions: Check for Extension Updates</i> command from the <i>VS Code Command Palette</i> to flag the
        extensions with available updates. Go through the list, check the repos and other resources for any reported issues,
        and update them if everything looks good.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the latest version of one of the outdated extensions is less than 7 days old (or whatever <Link
          url="https://nesbitt.io/2026/03/04/package-managers-need-to-cool-down.html">cooldown period</Link
        > you follow), open the dropdown menu next to <i>Uninstall</i> and choose <i>Install Specific Version...</i>, or
        skip it this time.
      </p>

      <p>Finally, update the pseudo-lockfile and repeat from time to time.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
